<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Parakeet.Engine.SileroVad</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    One project owns one model's interop, as Parakeet.Engine.ParakeetCpp owns parakeet.cpp's and
    Parakeet.Engine.Python owns the sidecar process. What this one owns is the Silero VAD graph
    on ONNX Runtime: Parakeet.Core declares ISpeechDetector and knows nothing about ONNX Runtime,
    the 576-sample window or the LSTM state, and a build target in Core fails if a NuGet reference
    ever appears there.

    ONNX Runtime is back in the .NET graph with this project — it left on 2026-08-21 when the
    diariser and the translator moved into the bundled Python. It is back for a model two
    megabytes large that has to score every frame inside the streaming segmenter, in process, on
    the CPU: a round trip to the sidecar per 32 ms window, or a whole-file pre-pass before the
    decode could start, would cost more than the model does. Directory.Packages.props says why
    1.29.0 and what not to call on OrtValue.

    Parakeet.Audio is referenced for Resampler: the segmenter runs at the recording's own rate and
    parakeet.cpp resamples inside the native library, so this is the one place on the
    transcription path that needs 16 kHz and has to make it for itself.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Parakeet.Core/Parakeet.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Parakeet.Audio/Parakeet.Audio.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.ML.OnnxRuntime" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Parakeet.Engine.SileroVad.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
